On Jul 7, 9:16 pm, Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:
> Let't try this another way. When the PC polymorphs
> into a green slime, PC brain polymorphs into slime
> neurons, PC muscle polymorphs into slime pseudopods,
> et cetera.
>
> Polymorphing is magic, and matches like with like,
> so you end up with a functioning, if icky green, PC.
>
> When the PC _gets slimed_, though, there's no
> particular motivation for the attacking green slime
> monster to turn the PC into a working kit of slime
> monster parts, so the PC just ends up as
> undifferentiated ooze.

All right, perhaps this makes better sense. But this also leads to
major inconsistencies.

1. If you polymorph into a green slime and attack other monsters, you
don't kill them outright (i.e. by turning them into undifferentiated
slime that just lies there), but rather turn them into functioning,
living, moving, complete green slimes. Same for if you use conflict
and a nearby green slime attacks other monsters.

2. As mentioned before, if a green slime attacks your pets, they turn
to living, functioning green slimes as well. Not only that, they stay
tame.

3. If being slimed just turns you to a big blobby pool of green slime,
then why on earth should your "body rise from the dead as a green
slime?" Now if the next adventurer came to your bones pile and found
"a green slime corpse" lying there, that might make a bit more sense.

Basically, it seems like a green slime attack acts like a polymorph
into green slime for everyone but the PC. Which is not only unfair but
also terribly illogical.

